

----------------------------------------------
ReentrantLock — What Is This?
A ReentrantLock is an advanced locking mechanism in Java used
to allow only one thread at a time to access critical code

It is a lock (key) for protecting shared resources.
If one thread takes the lock:
✅ it enters protected code
⏳ other threads wait until lock is released
------------------------------------------
Why Name “Reentrant”?

Because same thread can lock it multiple times without deadlocking itself.
Example:
Thread A lock once
Thread A lock again
Allowed
But it must unlock same number of times.

----------------------------------------------
Why It Came?

Because synchronized had limitations.
Problems with synchronized
1. No Try Lock
Cannot easily say:
Try now, if busy then do something else.

2. No Timeout Waiting
Cannot easily wait only 5 seconds.

3. No Fairness Control
Cannot choose FIFO waiting order.

4. No Interruptible Waiting
Harder to interrupt waiting thread cleanly.

5. Limited Advanced Features
Need condition variables and richer APIs.

ReentrantLock Solves This
Provides methods like:

lock()
unlock()
tryLock()
lockInterruptibly()
newCondition()
--------------------------------------
Real Example Why Needed

Suppose payment service wants lock:
If busy, don’t wait forever
Try for 2 seconds
If not available, return retry message
ReentrantLock supports this.
------------------------------------------------
What is Reentrancy?

Reentrancy means:
A thread that already holds a lock can enter again and acquire the same lock multiple times.
It does not block itself.

If thread already has room key, it can open same room again.
No need to wait outside.
------------------------------------------------------
Why Needed?
In real programs:
One method calls another method
Both methods use same lock
If reentrancy not supported:
❌ Same thread waits on its own lock
❌ Self-deadlock
---------------------------------
Real Life Example
Manager has office key.
He enters office.
Then enters inner cabin using same authority.
No need permission again.

example:
methodA() {
   lock();
   methodB();
}
methodB() {
   lock();   // same thread again
}
Because lock is reentrant:
✅ Allowed
----------------------------------------------
Reentrancy allows the same thread to acquire the same lock multiple times, maintaining a hold count.

Reentrancy prevents self-deadlock in nested synchronized/locked calls.
